      square values: [(0, 1), (7, 49), (16, 225), (25, 529), (34, 961)]  [5 hits, ~3.3 expected by chance]
  G(S): index where value = 36: [6];
      triangular values (first 200 terms): [(0, 0), (2, 6), (6, 36), (25, 528)]  [4 hits, ~4.1 expected by chance]
      square values: [(0, 0), (6, 36), (43, 1521)]  [3 hits, ~2.9 expected by chance]
  H(S): index where value = 36: [];
      triangular values (first 200 terms): [(0, 1), (3, 6), (7, 21), (11, 45), (28, 253)]  [5 hits, ~6.7 expected by chance]
      square values: [(0, 1), (2, 4), (4, 9), (6, 16), (43, 576)]  [5 hits, ~4.7 expected by chance]

  G(6) = 2+4+5+7+8+10 = 36: the six shortest dropping-word lengths A020914(1..6) add up to 36.
  No mechanism: G is a Beatty sum ~ alpha S^2/2 and hits a square/triangular number about as
  often as chance predicts.  LABEL: numerology.

  The only exact 'T_8' statement is the trivial one: #{(a,b) : a, b >= 0, a + b <= 7} = T_8 = 36,
  i.e. 36 is the number of 3-smooth numbers with at most 7 prime factors counted with multiplicity.
  check: 36

